{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And that it is a desire to the eyes.</b> For in every sin the good inclination has a dispute with the evil inclination: for the good inclination promises him the spiritual reward of the World to Come, and the evil inclination answers it and argues that it is better to pursue the desires that are tangible to the seeing eye, for the desires of this world are visible to every eye — as opposed to pursuing the delights of the World to Come, of which ״עַיִן לֹא רָאָתָה״ — eye has not seen. Therefore it says ״וַתֵּרֶא הָאִשָּׁה״ — the woman saw: she saw the words of the serpent and his arguments seemed right in her eyes — ״כִּי טוֹב הָעֵץ לְמַאֲכָל וְכִי תַאֲוָה הוּא לָעֵינַיִם״, that the desires of this world are visible eye to eye, to the exclusion of the delights of the World to Come, of which ״עַיִן לֹא רָאָתָה״. And one who wishes to lie will distance his witnesses (Rosh, Shevuot 6:13)." "<b>And she gave also to her husband with her.</b> The word ״עִמָּהּ״ — the commentators explained: so that he would be with her always and would not take another when she dies. And it is possible that she gave it to him while he was literally with her, for then she pressed him in the manner stated regarding Samson (Judges 16:16) ״וַתְּאַלְצֵהוּ״ — and she pressed him. For at another time perhaps he would not have listened to her to transgress the word of HaShem, and concerning this he said in his defense ״הָאִשָּׁה אֲשֶׁר נָתַתָּ עִמָּדִי״ — the woman whom You gave with me: at the time she was literally with me, she prevailed over me. For according to the plain sense there is no sense to this defense. And what he argued, ״הִיא נָתְנָה לִּי מִן הָעֵץ״ — she gave me from the tree — perhaps he intended to say, by way of defense: since in the commandment it was not said ״מִפְּרִי עֵץ הַדַּעַת לֹא תֹאכַל״ — from the fruit of the tree of knowledge you shall not eat — but rather ״מֵעֵץ הַדַּעַת לֹא תֹאכַל״ — from the tree of knowledge you shall not eat — I thought that the intention was that I am not permitted to detach the fruit from the tree; but if it is already detached and standing there, I thought it was permissible for me to eat it, and now she gave me from the tree — and I did not take it from the tree myself." "And there are those who say that he said: since You gave me the woman with me to cook and to prepare all the needs of the household, I assumed as her established practice that she would not feed me anything forbidden; therefore I thought that this fruit was from a different tree."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:26:54.810296521Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}